itten using the currency code PhP?\", \"PhP{total_rw}c\", \"Remember that pesos use ₱ or PhP, and centavos can be written as a decimal.\", \"{chunk}-{v}\", \"{num_to_words} pesos\", \"{p0}\", \"{p0} {p1}\", \"{total_rw}¢\", \"{v} hundred\", \"{words} centavos\", \"{words} pesos\", \"{member}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"Empty members: the _ONES and _TENS lists both have an empty first member. F13 and F11 can therefore print nothing, F14 can print a bare space or a bare tens word, and F17 can print the word hundred with no number before it. Those empty values reach keyed answers and distractors through F19, F12, F18 and F03, giving keyed answers such as a blank word followed by pesos, or hundred pesos with no number. Beyond the empty values, F17 with _ONES members gives hundreds up to nineteen hundred, and F12, F14 and F28 print amounts above ₱100 as answer choices, outside the up_to_100 range. Unlinked words: F03 and F19 take their words from F13 and F17 with no derived_from_values, so the amount named in the text need not match the total the problem shows. Centavo amounts: F18 prints the word centavos, F16 prints the cent sign, F07 prints a c suffix, and F28 has centavo members. The lc excludes centavo coins, and the packet's NOT_YET_KNOWN list includes centavo, so these are out of scope. The packet also lists centavo in introduces_vocab, which contradicts NOT_YET_KNOWN; I applied NOT_YET_KNOWN as the gate. F09 teaches that centavos can be written as a decimal. Decimal appears in no vocabulary list and is beyond Grade 1 Q4, so F09 is out of scope. F05 asks for the currency code PhP, and currency code appears in no vocabulary list and is a concept beyond this lc. The remaining families F01, F02, F04, F06, F08, F10, F15, F20, F21, F22, F23, F24, F25, F26 and F27 print in-range peso or coin text with no gate breach that I could find.\"}",
